1. Whole-function hot/cold section classification used the summary's
   percentile is_hot/is_cold, which collapses on a skewed unit: for
   [3x1.2M, 10x40K, 1] the 90%-cumulative threshold lands exactly at 40K,
   so every 1%-execution helper cleared 'c >= hot_threshold' and would have
   been placed in .text.hot — the opposite of the I-cache goal. Section
   splitting now uses a dedicated, stricter ratio: HOT iff the entry count
   reaches 10% of the unit's hottest function, COLD iff it is 1000x below
   the hottest. Inlining decisions keep the percentile is_hot/is_cold
   (their aggressiveness is correct there); only I-cache placement tightens.

2. layout_module skipped functions with < 2 blocks entirely, so a unit of
   tiny cold helpers never got sectioned. Single-block functions now still
   get the whole-function hot/cold section classification (the safe i-cache
   win); only intra-function layout requires >= 2 blocks.

3. Trivial: remove a doubled semicolon in the value-profile recorder.

Validated on a dispatcher workload (3 hot + 10 cold handlers + cold main):
.text.hot now contains exactly the hot trio, .text.unlikely contains main,
the 40K helpers stay in .text. Regression 144 passed / 0 failed, correctness
50/50, intrinsics 3/3.
